{
  "_meta": {
    "name": "SpeakMirror English Lexicon",
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "created": "2026-09-05",
    "description": "SpeakMirror English practice lexicon for the rule engine: tiered fillers, hedging words, vague-to-precise mappings. Fully self-authored for this project; no third-party word lists were used.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "sources": [
      {
        "name": "SpeakMirror self-authored (2026-09-05)",
        "license": "MIT (shipped with this project)",
        "usage": "All entries hand-curated for spoken-English practice feedback; quality over quantity, no filler padding."
      }
    ],
    "notes": [
      "Matching contract: pure-ASCII entries (including apostrophes as in \"don't\" and spaces as in \"you know\") must match on word boundaries (equivalent to \\b semantics) and case-insensitively; multi-word phrases are matched as whole phrases with per-word boundaries. Longest-first precedence is unchanged (\"sort of\" wins over \"so\").",
      "Filler tiers: high = always counted and flagged; medium = flagged only after the per-minute threshold is reached (prefer under-reporting over noise). Entries with high false-positive risk in normal grammar (so, well) are deliberately kept in medium.",
      "\"right?\" includes the question mark so it only matches the tag-question filler, never the adjective \"right\".",
      "Some entries intentionally appear in more than one field (\"kind of\" / \"sort of\" are both fillers and hedges; \"a bit\" is both a hedge and a vagueToPrecise key) — different rules read different fields, mirroring the Chinese lexicon design.",
      "English does not get emotion-words / time-vague / imagery / golden-quote rule data: those rules are Chinese-specific heuristics and are skipped for English sentences."
    ],
    "counts": {
      "fillers_high": 17,
      "fillers_medium": 19,
      "hedges": 27,
      "vagueToPrecise": 76
    }
  },

